Z||000000||kslish||05-11-2001||11:26 PM||||So I finally get the interior back in the Fiero I'm taking to Carlisle and I'm cruising around locally to get some gas.  As I come to a stop at an intersection, the brake pedal sinks to the floor!  Fortunately I'm only a few blocks from home and I make it back okay.  At first I thought it was the master cylinder, but I finally found brake fluid dripping from the front of the car.  It seems I have a pinhole leak in one of the lines a little below where the lines pass through the spare tire well to underneath the car.  I need to get this fixed before Carlisle and I plan on leaving it up to a pro to get it done quickly since even though I do most of my own mechanical work including brakes and calipers, I don't have a clue about replacing these lines.  I need some advice on the following:<P>1.) The master cylinder reservoir is low in the front half of the reservoir.  This means that it's a front line that is leaking correct?<P>2.) Is it better to get pre-formed lines or custom bent ones?  Should I prefer one over the other?  A local old-time mechanic that works on musclecars and hot-rods says he can replace both of my front lines for around $100 with custom bent ones he'll make from new straight tubing using the old lines as a template.<P>3.) Is $100 a good price?  I assume that it is mostly labor since I assume the tubing itself is pretty cheap.<P>4.) Is there anything else I may need to know before I get this done that I may not have thought of?<P>Thanks,<BR>Ken S.<BR>'87GT and '86SC<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||1
